Looking at this problem, the key issue is load imbalance due to the heavy-tailed row length distribution. Let me analyze the previous answer's weaknesses:

schedule(dynamic, 64) — chunk size 64 is too small for a skewed distribution. Dynamic scheduling with tiny chunks has high overhead, and a single "hot" row (up to 20000 entries) can dominate one chunk. The heavy-tailed rows are scattered, so small chunks force frequent synchronization.

Parallel region inside the reps loop — the parallel region is recreated every rep (20 times), which adds fork/join overhead. Moving the reps loop inside the parallel region amortizes thread creation.

No NUMA/thread-affinity consideration but more importantly, x is read-only and heavily reused; it should be accessed efficiently.

Key improvements:

Hoist the parallel region outside the reps loop so we fork/join once, not 20 times.

Use schedule(guided) — guided scheduling starts with large chunks and decreases, which handles heavy-tailed distributions much better than fixed dynamic chunks, with lower overhead than small dynamic chunks. This lets the big rows get claimed early.

Consider schedule(static) with cyclic distribution for the hot rows. Actually guided with a reasonable minimum chunk is best here.

Prefetch / restrict pointers to help the compiler vectorize the inner dot product (though indirect col[k] gathers limit this).

Let me also think: guided scheduling with default min chunk (1) can be problematic — a few huge rows could still be one thread's. But guided hands out large chunks first, so a thread grabbing the first chunk (which contains many rows) processes them; the heavy rows are randomly distributed. guided, 8 or so balances overhead vs. granularity well.

Actually, the best strategy for heavy-tailed: dynamic scheduling with a modest chunk (e.g., 32) is common, but guided with min chunk ~16 typically beats it because it reduces scheduling overhead while still balancing.

Let me also hoist the parallel region out of the reps loop. Since y is written each rep and read only at the end, and each thread writes disjoint y[i], there's no cross-thread dependency between reps. But we need all reps to complete before reading y — the barrier at end of parallel region handles that.

